  "slug": "gene-rna-d705",
  "summary": "New preprints link RNA solubility, splicing delay and piRNA-dependent transposon silencing to oocyte, embryo and spermatocyte gene expression control.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is post-transcriptional and RNA-centered control of gene expression in germline and early developmental contexts. In zebrafish, fractionation RNA-seq maps RNA insolubility during oocyte-to-embryo transition and links it to ribosome, poly(A) tail and m6A features. In Drosophila spermatocytes, co-transcriptional splicing of Y-loop A transcripts is delayed in a cluster-dependent manner, with delay increasing as cells mature. In mouse germ cells, COPS5 loss lowers piRNA pathway gene expression, reduces pachytene piRNAs, derepresses LINE1 and IAP, and arrests spermatogenesis. Related work covers histone mRNA 3' misprocessing after rRNA biogenesis disruption in C. elegans, exosome surveillance of PROMPTs in mouse embryonic stem cells, Marf1 in the Drosophila accessory gland, and GAR1 in snoRNP-dependent lipid metabolism, extending the theme beyond the germline.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
